{
 "@context": "https://real-life.org/meta/v1/context.jsonld",
 "@graph": [
  {
   "@id": "rlnp:Mensch",
   "skos:relatedMatch": "rltp:Anchor",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately no exactMatch: RLTP has no person object, only anchors per context. This is by design (relationships never converge into a person)."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rlnp:Mensch",
   "skos:relatedMatch": "rls:profile",
   "skos:note": "The profile is the view of a person, not the person."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rls:connector",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP and RLTP. The connector is pure Stack construction."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rlnp:Kreis",
   "skos:note": "Converged 2026-09-18: a circle is the thing itself; the Stack constructs it as a space, the Trust Protocol as a group. A circle chooses its own form and access, and the space carries exactly that: publicly visible, open for anyone to post, or by invitation. \"Space\" in the RLNP text is a reference to the Stack space, not a term of its own.",
   "skos:exactMatch": [
    "rls:space",
    "rltp:Group"
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "rls:space",
   "skos:exactMatch": "rltp:Group"
  },
  {
   "@id": "rlnp:Sichtbarkeit",
   "skos:relatedMatch": [
    "rltp:VisibilityMode",
    "rls:multi-space-item"
   ],
   "rl:convergesWith": "rls:visibility",
   "skos:note": "Three reaches, three constructions: \"within one circle\" is RLTP private; \"within several circles\" is the Stack's item in several spaces (the RLTP connector builds it as a mirror); \"published\" is RLTP open at the origin, delivery of open data needs another backend (multi-connector). VisibilityMode covers the first and third only."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rlnp:Verifikation",
   "skos:exactMatch": [
    "rls:verification",
    "rltp:Enactment"
   ],
   "skos:relatedMatch": "rltp:EncounterCredential",
   "skos:note": "One act in three parts (2026-09-18): the encounter happens in the network, the Stack verifies it in the app, the Trust Protocol constructs that verification as an enactment; the encounter credential is its result. Socially it happens once per relationship; the protocol allows repeated enactments, each with a fresh anchor, all landing on the same edge."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rlnp:Bezeugen",
   "skos:relatedMatch": "rltp:EncounterCredential",
   "rl:convergesWith": "rltp:Attestation",
   "skos:note": "Target: a general attestation type in RLTP (candidate: DTG Endorsement Credential). Until then only the encounter has an artifact."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Member",
   "skos:exactMatch": "rls:member",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without a counterpart in RLNP (2026-09-18): the network knows membership only as what it must not require. Belonging to the network lives in relationships, not in lists; a circle chooses its own form and access, and membership is one of the forms the Stack and the Trust Protocol provide for it."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rlnp:Rolle",
   "rl:convergesWith": "rltp:Attestation",
   "skos:note": "A role taken on is a witnessed promise (RLNP 6.18, 8.13). Depends on the attestation type."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rlnp:Beziehung",
   "skos:narrowMatch": "rltp:Edge",
   "rl:falseFriend": "rls:relation",
   "skos:note": "Decided 2026-09-18 (for now): an RLNP relationship is broader than an RLTP edge. Two kinds: person–person is an edge from an encounter (RLTP Edge); person–agent is an edge from appointment or vouching (future attestation). Proposal to RLNP 6.2: name both kinds. Relation (Stack) links items, not people: false friend.",
   "rl:convergesWith": "rltp:Attestation"
  },
  {
   "@id": "rls:verification",
   "skos:exactMatch": "rltp:Enactment"
  },
  {
   "@id": "rlnp:Begegnung",
   "skos:relatedMatch": "rlnp:Verifikation",
   "skos:note": "The event itself, repeated and ordinary, deliberately without counterpart in the Stack or the Trust Protocol: the encounter belongs to the people. Verification is the one-time act that records the relationship; later encounters need none."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rls:mirror",
   "skos:relatedMatch": "rls:multi-space-item",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or RLTP: for the person it is simply a thing in several spaces; the mirror exists because the Trust Protocol partitions storage by access boundary. The protocol itself knows only groups and their replication."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:PairAnchor",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the fresh anchor a person uses for one enactment is pure protocol construction; for the person it is still the one relationship (RLNP Beziehung, mapped at rltp:Edge)."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:ContactCard",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the card carries what the counterpart needs to reach and recognize an anchor; the Stack shows a profile, which is the view of a person, not a delivery address."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Challenge",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the fresh value each party shows during an enactment is a construction detail of the verification (mapped at rltp:Enactment)."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Ceremony",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the registered form of an enactment (how the two parties see each other's challenges) is a construction detail of the verification (mapped at rltp:Enactment)."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Step",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: one party's confirmation inside an enactment is a construction detail of the verification; the Stack and RLNP see the whole act."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:EnactmentBinding",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the digest that ties the two challenges together is construction, invisible to the person."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:EnactmentRecord",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: what a party keeps after its own step is local state of the protocol, not a thing in the network."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:CredentialDigest",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the content digest of an encounter credential is construction, invisible to the person."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Bundle",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the delivery unit of the one-scan path is construction of the transport, invisible to the person."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:IncomingEdge",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: a direction state of rltp:Edge (only the counterpart has confirmed). For the person it is an unfinished verification; the Stack shows it as pending, not as a relationship."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:OutgoingEdge",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: a direction state of rltp:Edge (only this party has confirmed). For the person it is an unfinished verification; the Stack shows it as pending, not as a relationship."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:MutualEdge",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the state of rltp:Edge in which both parties have confirmed; this is the state RLNP Beziehung maps to."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:AuthorityPort",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the part of the Access Layer that decides membership, policy and authorization is internal protocol architecture. The person sees a circle and its members; the Stack sees a space (rltp:Group)."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:KeyPort",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the interface through which an adapter produces epoch secrets is internal protocol architecture, invisible to the person and below the Stack's connector."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Healing",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the key port's correction of its key structure to the materialized membership is internal key management. For the person and the Stack membership simply holds."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:MemberAnchor",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the per-group anchor under which a member acts is protocol construction, like every anchor (RLNP Mensch, related at rltp:Anchor). For the person it is still the one member of the circle (rls:member, mapped at rltp:Member)."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:CommunityAnchor",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: an ordinary member anchor that a person uses as a chosen cross-relationship coordinate is protocol construction, like every anchor (RLNP Mensch, related at rltp:Anchor). The Stack shows a profile, which is the view of a person, not an anchor."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:DeviceCard",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the signed binding of a device's key material to a member anchor is protocol construction. Neither RLNP nor the Stack distinguish a person's devices."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Service",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: a party that stores and forwards a group's items without holding its keys is protocol infrastructure. The network knows only people and circles; the Stack's connector is the app-side access to a backend, not a party the group registers."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:ServiceClass",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: what a service knows of a group (blind, view, log) is a registered protocol statement about rltp:Service, invisible to the person and the Stack."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:AuthorityLog",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the append-only operation DAG that is the sole source of authorization state is protocol construction. The person sees who belongs to the circle; the Stack sees the members of a space."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Operation",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: a signed, causally anchored envelope in the authority log is protocol construction, invisible to the person and the Stack."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Materialization",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the deterministic derivation of group state from the log is protocol construction. The person and the Stack see only its result: the circle or space with its members."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Canonical",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: whether materialization accepts an operation at its causal position is a property inside the protocol's conflict resolution, invisible to the person and the Stack."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:ReplicaBoundary",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the set of parties entitled to hold the group's replicated log is protocol construction. RLNP Sichtbarkeit is about what a person shows to whom, not about who stores the log."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:MaterializedState",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the group state yielded by materialization is protocol construction; the person sees the circle, the Stack the space (rltp:Group), not a derived state."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:ForkedState",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: a fail-closed outcome of materialization and a narrower case of rltp:MaterializedState, arising when a policy change or dissolution runs concurrent with an enforcement operation. It is conflict handling inside the protocol, invisible to the person and the Stack."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:RetainedMembers",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the computed set of members an enforcement operation's new epoch secret reaches is key-management construction. For the person and the Stack the circle simply keeps its members (rls:member, mapped at rltp:Member)."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:PendingExit",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the state between a merged member.leave and its discharging transition is protocol construction. For the person leaving a circle is one act; the Stack has no intermediate state for it."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Policy",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: group-defined data stating which proof satisfies the group's decision rule is protocol construction. RLNP lets a circle choose its own form and decisions without encoding them as rules; the Stack has no policy object."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:PolicyProof",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the signer set and vouch set an operation is judged by is construction of rltp:Policy, invisible to the person and the Stack."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:KeyServiceDuty",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the members' standing obligation to (re)deliver current-epoch key material is a duty inside the protocol's key management. For the person and the Stack entitled members simply can read."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Epoch",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: a numbered period of the group's key world, aligned with MLS, is key-management construction. Neither RLNP nor the Stack divide a circle or space into key periods."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:EpochKeyLineage",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the chain of per-transition entries by which the adapter linear/0.1 keeps earlier content keys readable is construction of one adapter (rltp:Adapter), invisible to the person and the Stack."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:AuthorizationView",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the quorum-signed object by which a service of class view learns a group's epoch and authorized devices is protocol infrastructure (rltp:Service), invisible to the person and the Stack."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:PrivilegedOperation",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: an operation of the closed catalog and a narrower case of rltp:Operation, each with its class, epoch effect and body profile. Admitting or removing a member is one act for the person and the Stack; the operation is its construction."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:ImplicitCapability",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: the read/write standing every member holds by membership alone is how the protocol states that belonging is enough. The Stack expresses it through membership itself (rls:member, mapped at rltp:Member); RLNP knows no membership list to attach it to."
  },
  {
   "@id": "rltp:Adapter",
   "skos:note": "Deliberately without counterpart in RLNP or the Stack: a registered binding of a key-agreement procedure to the key port is internal protocol architecture, invisible to the person. The Stack's backend binding is the connector, which is pure Stack construction and unrelated."
  }
 ]
}
